ZTEXT0010000150586a4f9b80000000
Zero independent volumes and paths

The public naming contract is A: through Z:, in discovery order. Each volume
has its own root, written A:|. GO B:| selects B: as the current root. Lowercase
go and lowercase drive letters are accepted too. Relative names use that
selected volume; a qualified name selects only the target of that operation.

Examples inside the Zero terminal:
  disks
  GO A:|
  create note
  save note Hello from Zero
  read A:|note
  watch B:|
  save B:|second Another volume
  search A:|n*
  del B:|second

The separator is the vertical bar, ASCII124. Slash and backslash are not
aliases. CD is not a command. Current file operations address flat root files;
GO into nested directories is planned and rejected explicitly for now.
Names use letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen, at most31 bytes.
Dot and dot-dot are not ordinary file names. Terminal text is at most400 bytes.
write prints text; save writes a disk file; create preserves an existing file.
del removes one file; rm remains a compatibility alias. search lists matching
filenames with * for any number of characters and ? for one character. A
plain search name is an exact match. watch . and GO . use the current root.
Zero and Ezero programs share these file operations through the native guest
service, including persistent disk writes. See portability/guest/ABI.ztext.
Stored ASCII text may include TAB, LF and CR. read automatically validates
and decodes .ztext files (or files with the ZTEXT001 header), displaying only
the payload after length and checksum checks pass. Its32-byte header leaves
368 payload bytes within the current400-byte file limit. Non-ASCII payloads
are rejected for terminal display; raw file primitives retain the full bytes.

An independent volume is not a directory mounted under another volume.
Physical disks and volumes are different: the disk layer identifies devices,
the discovery layer describes whole-volume or partition extents, and a format
driver handles filesystem metadata within that extent. Disk devices are shown
by disks; there is no Linux-style /dev directory containing user file trees.

Hardware scope
The kernel scans four legacy IDE ATA positions: primary master/slave and
secondary master/slave. It requires LBA28,512-byte logical sectors and FLUSH
CACHE. Optical ATAPI media are not writable data volumes. SATA AHCI, NVMe,
USB storage and sector formats other than512 bytes are future drivers.
The addressable size is at most268435456 sectors (128GiB) per physical disk.
disks reports whole MiB rounded down, underlying physical disk number and
recognized filesystem. Unsupported/corrupt devices or volumes are identified.

Letters currently follow physical position and partition discovery order,
including unknown filesystem volumes. They are not persistent disk IDs and
can change if attachment order or partition tables change. Persistent IDs and
saved letter assignments are planned. No automatic partition resize, format,
repair or destructive conversion occurs during discovery.
Discovery supports raw volumes, four primary MBR partitions and GPT revision1
with up to128 entries of128 bytes. GPT primary/backup headers and tables are
verified, including CRCs, geometry, unique IDs and nonoverlapping partitions.
The GPT read-only attribute is honored. Extended MBR chains and hybrid tables
are refused. See fs/discovery-profile.ztext for the exact recognition rules.

The release supplies only the read-only ISO boot image. A writable disk must
be created separately in the user's VM manager or preserved from an earlier
ZeroFS setup. Attach that dedicated disk as primary IDE master; it is normally
A:|. A new blank disk is discovered as an unknown raw volume. Confirm its
identity with disks, select GO A:|, then explicitly initialize it with
format ZERO. This command erases the selected disk's ZeroFS area; never use
it as a recovery step for existing files. No formatting happens at boot.
Without a writable disk, the OS can run its terminal and graphics but cannot
save persistent user files. The18-sector ZeroFS format and eight-file capacity
remain unchanged, even on a larger virtual disk. format ZERO applies only to
the selected dedicated raw primary-master disk, never a foreign or partitioned
volume.
Other recognized volumes dispatch to original Zero ext4, NTFS or Btrfs state
machines. Their detailed feature restrictions are in fs/*-profile.txt.
No ext4, NTFS or Btrfs disk image is bundled. Existing user-owned volumes in
the supported profiles may be attached separately; see COMPATIBLE-DISKS.ztext.

All requests are bounded by the physical disk and selected volume. Metadata
is revalidated for each operation; switching letters cannot reuse another
volume's cache. An integrity, recovery or I/O failure blocks further foreign
volume writes until restart/recovery. Successful mutation includes the driver's
documented flush protocol. A flush is not a guarantee of crash atomicity;
see each filesystem's transaction and recovery limitations.

Development evidence is recorded in verification/volumes and the filesystem
verification directories. verification.ztext describes the final integrated ISO
and completed VM matrix; a host-driver test alone is not a full-system boot.

Visible physical disk numbers start at1 (ATA slot0 is displayed as disk1).
Volume letters and stored sector addresses are unchanged.
